  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nWhile mid-level clouds have been encroaching on our area from\nSD, low-level dry air has largely kept any rain from reaching \nthe surface. Virga will be expected this afternoon and evening \nas the clouds move east across MN/WI, but did keep 10% PoPs in \nthe forecast in case of a sprinkle. Lows tonight will not be \nnearly as cool as last night, owing to the greater cloud cover \nand an incoming warmer air mass ahead of a shortwave. 40s are \nexpected for most places except for our far eastern WI counties \nwhere mid to upper 30s are more likely. Highs Saturday will be a\nfew degrees warmer than today, though cloud cover will increase\nagain in the afternoon as the aforementioned shortwave \napproaches. CAMs favor an area of showers developing from \ncentral to central to southern MN during the early to mid \nafternoon, which then travels east into WI by evening. Rainfall \namounts currently don't look to be too heavy, though 0.1- 0.2\" \nis a possibility. A localized area could potentially receive \nmore if a stray thunderstorm occurs. If you have outdoor \nactivities planned on Saturday, it would be a good idea to have \nan umbrella or shelter to dodge the rain. Winds will also be \nbreezy Saturday afternoon out of the south as a LLJ advances \nahead of the mid-level trough. Gusts will generally reach 25-30 \nmph across MN with isolated gusts of 35+ mph possible near the \nBuffalo Ridge. The winds will turn northwesterly and slow as we \ngo into Saturday night.\n\nAfter the rain and shortwave moves east during Saturday night,\nthe forecast looks very quiet for most of next week. Long-\nrange deterministic and ensemble guidance shows the upper-level \njetstream remaining mainly off to our north. This should allow \nany shortwaves and their forcing to pass well away from our CWA,\ngreatly limiting our chances for precipitation. Temperatures \nwill slightly cool Sunday and Monday following a cold frontal \npassage Saturday night. Highs during this time are forecast to \nbe in the low to mid 60s. The best chance for frost in the \nforecast period will be Sunday night when lows could dip into \nthe mid 30s in eastern MN and northwest WI. Afterwards, \ntemperatures will warm to above normal (highs in the mid 60s to \nlower 70s) through next Friday as broad ridging builds over the \nwestern half of the CONUS. The first full week of October looks \ngreat if you have any outdoor activities planned!",
